Under IRS rules, a SIMPLE IRA charges a 25% early withdrawal penalty during your first two years of participation before dropping to 10%, whereas a 401(k) charges 10% before age 59½. A 401(k) also allows penalty-free withdrawals if you leave your employer at age 55 or older, an exception that does not apply to a SIMPLE IRA. Standard IRS penalty exceptions, such as disability, still apply to early distributions from both a SIMPLE IRA and a 401(k).